"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][6]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M6-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M6-L1 – Population, sample and selection mechanisms<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Bachelor: Statistical inference and experimental design (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L3<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish the target\npopulation from the sampling frame and recognise selection bias in an\nestimation problem.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Which unit should be represented?<\/h2>\n<p>An energy supplier wants to determine the average number of restarts\nof a smart meter per month. The target population comprises all 1,000\ndevices operated in January. A device is the unit of investigation; a\nrestart is an event within that unit. The sampling frame is the register\nfrom which devices can actually be selected. If older devices without\nremote maintenance are absent from it, even random selection from this\nregister may fail to represent the entire target population.<\/p>\n<p>A parameter describes the target population, whereas a statistic is\ncalculated from observed data. For a finite population with values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe target parameter is the mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msubsup><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>N<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=N^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^N y_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn estimate is meaningful only once the population, time and\ncharacteristic have been clearly defined.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Selection and weighting<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001715_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Populationsanteile 80 zu 20 Prozent, Stichprobenanteile 50 zu 50; gewichtetes Mittel 3,2 statt 5.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Populationsanteile 80 zu 20 Prozent,\nStichprobenanteile 50 zu 50; gewichtetes Mittel 3,2 statt\n5.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Stratified sampling.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>In simple random sampling without replacement from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunits, every set of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunits has the same selection probability. In stratified sampling, the\npopulation is divided into disjoint groups and samples are drawn\nindependently within each group. This can ensure adequate representation\nof small but important groups. Cluster sampling, by contrast, selects\nentire groups, such as buildings containing several meters; observations\nwithin a building may be strongly related.<\/p>\n<p>In the teaching example, 800 devices are new and 200 are old. Twenty\ndevices are randomly selected from each age group. Their observed group\nmeans are 2 and 8 restarts respectively. The unweighted mean is 5; the\npopulation-weighted result is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>μ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.2<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat\\mu=0.8\\cdot2+0.2\\cdot8=3.2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The weights correspond to the proportions of the strata, not their\nproportions in the sample. Sampling error remains; the graphic shows the\ndifferent compositions, not the uncertainty. If the sizes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare known, the general expression is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>μ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat\\mu=\\sum_h (N_h/N)\\bar y_h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout suitable assumptions about selection, weighting cannot correct\nunknown differences.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Systematic errors<\/h2>\n<p>Self-selection arises, for example, when only operators experiencing\nproblems respond to a form. Non-response can occur after random\nselection. Both mechanisms can bias the results; simply obtaining more\nresponses does not solve the problem. Record the inclusion rules,\nnon-responses and time of data collection. Check whether a statement is\njustified only for responding devices or for the entire target\npopulation. Independence must be assessed at the level of the unit of\ninvestigation; ten measurements from one device do not replace ten\nindependent devices.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>An installation contains 90% type A and 10% type B; both types occur\nequally often in a random sample drawn within the strata. The group\nmeans are 4 and 14. Calculate both overall means.<\/p>\n<p>Solution: Unweighted\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>14<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4+14)/2=9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nstratified\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.9<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>14<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.9\\cdot4+0.1\\cdot14=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe second value accounts for the actual composition of the\ninstallation. Standard errors require knowledge of group sizes,\nvariability within groups and the sampling design.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.openintro.org/book/os/\">OpenIntro Statistics:\nChapters 1, 5, 6 and 7 (chapter overview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M6-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M6-L2 – Point estimation, bias and standard error<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Bachelor: Statistical inference and experimental design (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L4<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L5<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L7<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish unbiasedness,\nvariance, mean squared error and standard error.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A rule and its observed value<\/h2>\n<p>An estimator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(X_1,\\ldots,X_n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a function of random data; after a particular data collection it\nyields the estimate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSuppose the unknown mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the quantity of interest. Under independent, identically distributed\nobservations with finite variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe sample mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas the properties<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[\\bar X]=\\mu,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The standard error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">SE<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{SE}(\\bar X)=\\sigma/\\sqrt n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes the variability of the estimator across repeated samples. It\nis not the spread of individual observations. When\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis unknown,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s/\\sqrt n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis often used;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the sample standard deviation based on the variance estimate with\ndenominator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Error across repetitions<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001696_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Breite, bei 10 zentrierte Verteilung A und schmale, um eins verschobene Verteilung B; B hat den kleineren MSE.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Breite, bei 10 zentrierte Verteilung A\nund schmale, um eins verschobene Verteilung B; B hat den kleineren\nMSE.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Bias and precision.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For a scalar target\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=E[T]-\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes the bias. The mean squared error decomposes as<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">MSE<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>b<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{MSE}(T)=E[(T-\\theta)^2]=\\operatorname{Var}(T)+b^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This follows because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T-\\theta=(T-E[T])+b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\non squaring, the cross term has expectation zero. An unbiased estimator\ncan therefore be imprecise, while a biased estimator can have lower MSE.\nThe graphic shows two hypothetical sampling distributions for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nA has mean 10 and standard deviation 2, while B has mean 11 and standard\ndeviation 0.5. A has MSE 4 and B has MSE 1.25. This does not establish a\ngeneral preference for biased estimators; the comparison depends on the\nparameter, loss function and sampling model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Precision and sample size<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose the standard deviation in a study of mean processing time is\n12 ms. Under the independent model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>36<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=36<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives a standard error of 2 ms and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>144<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=144<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives one of 1 ms. Four times as many independent observations halve the\nstandard error. By contrast, a constant calibration error of 3 ms does\nnot disappear with more measurements. Consistency means that the\nestimator approaches the target in probability as sample size grows.\nUnbiasedness alone does not guarantee this; an unbiased estimator that\nuses only the first observation typically does not become more\nprecise.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>One estimator has bias −2 and variance 3; another is unbiased with\nvariance 8. Which has the smaller mean squared error?<\/p>\n<p>Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3+(-2)^2=7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncompared with 8. The first is preferable under this loss function. The\nunit of MSE is the square of the parameter’s unit; its square root\n(RMSE) has the same unit as the target.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat415/Lesson02\">Penn State\nSTAT 415: Estimation (Part I), Lesson 2<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.openintro.org/book/os/\">OpenIntro Statistics:\nChapters 1, 5, 6 and 7 (chapter overview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M6-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M6-L3 – Maximum likelihood and the method of moments<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Bachelor: Statistical inference and experimental design (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L5<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2<\/a>\nto <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L5<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Construct likelihoods and moment\nequations and compare estimation methods.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hold the data fixed and vary the parameter<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001524_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Kurve p³(1−p)² auf null bis eins mit Maximum bei 0,6 und Nullstellen an beiden Rändern.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Kurve p³(1−p)² auf null bis eins mit\nMaximum bei 0,6 und Nullstellen an beiden Rändern.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Maximum of the likelihood.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A diagnostic program returns the values 1, 0, 1, 1, 0 in five\nindependent runs under the same conditions (1 denotes success). Under a\nBernoulli model with unknown success probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe probability of exactly this ordered sequence is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L(p)=p^3(1-p)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nViewed as a function of the parameter with the data fixed, it is called\nthe likelihood. It is not a probability distribution over\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\na Bayesian approach would additionally require a prior distribution.<\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;p&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mi>p<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell(p)=\\log L(p)=3\\log p+2\\log(1-p),\\qquad \\ell&#39;(p)=\\frac{3}{p}-\\frac{2}{1-p}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The derivative is zero at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat p=3/5=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell&#39;&#39;(p)=-3/p^2-2/(1-p)^2&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the likelihood is zero at the boundaries, this is the global\nmaximum. The graphic traces\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L(p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover the parameter space. If all trials succeed or all fail, however,\nthe maximum lies at the boundary\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrespectively; searching only for interior stationary points is then\ninsufficient.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Products, logarithms and optimisation<\/h2>\n<p>For independent data with density or probability mass function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∏<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L(\\theta)=\\prod_i f_\\theta(x_i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe log-likelihood is a sum and reduces numerical underflow problems.\nFor continuous values, the product is of densities, not of probabilities\nat individual points. Numerical methods often minimise the negative\nlog-likelihood. Parameter bounds, boundary solutions, model\nidentifiability and possible local optima must be checked\nseparately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Method of moments and model choice<\/h2>\n<p>The method of moments equates empirical moments to model moments. For\nthe Bernoulli model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[X]=p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nimmediately gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x=\\hat p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe same result as maximum likelihood. For the uniform distribution on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0,\\theta]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhowever,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[X]=\\theta/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor invented measurements 1, 2 and 6,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>θ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat\\theta_{\\mathrm{MM}}=2\\bar x=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor measurements 1, 1 and 7, the result is also 6, although one\nmeasurement would then lie outside the estimated support. For positive\ndata, the likelihood method selects the sample maximum, since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>θ<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L(\\theta)=\\theta^{-n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta\\ge\\max_i x_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand zero otherwise. The difference illustrates that algebraically\nmatching moments do not guarantee a valid model fit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Four independent Bernoulli trials yield exactly one success. Write\ndown the log-likelihood and determine its maximum.<\/p>\n<p>Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell(p)=\\log p+3\\log(1-p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith derivative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/p-3/(1-p)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe second derivative is negative, and the likelihood is zero at the\nboundaries. The method of moments also yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat415/Lesson02\">Penn State\nSTAT 415: Estimation (Part I), Lesson 2<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat415/Lesson03\">Penn State\nSTAT 415: Estimation (Part II), Lesson 3<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M6-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M6-L4 – Confidence intervals and hypothesis tests<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Bachelor: Statistical inference and experimental design (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L7<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate a t-interval and\ninterpret p-values and coverage probability correctly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Intervals offer a repeated-sampling guarantee<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001608_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Zwölf Intervalle um unterschiedliche Schätzwerte; eines schließt den wahren Mittelwert 50 nicht ein und ist rot markiert.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Zwölf Intervalle um unterschiedliche\nSchätzwerte; eines schließt den wahren Mittelwert 50 nicht ein und ist\nrot markiert.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Confidence intervals across repetitions.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A workshop estimates the mean duration of a testing step. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent normally distributed measurements with unknown variance, the\ntwo-sided t-confidence interval for the mean is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>±<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mfrac><mi>s<\/mi><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x\\pm t_{1-\\alpha/2,n-1}\\frac{s}{\\sqrt n}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_{q,\\nu}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nquantile of the t-distribution with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ν<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndegrees of freedom. Under the stated assumptions, across many\nrepetitions an average of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100(1-\\alpha)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper cent of intervals constructed in this way contain the fixed true\nparameter. A particular interval either contains it or does not; without\nan additional probability model for the parameter, this is not a 95%\nprobability for the parameter. The graphic illustrates this distinction\nwith hypothetical intervals.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>An example in seconds<\/h2>\n<p>Nine independent measurements give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>52<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x=52<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns. The standard error is 2 s. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand eight degrees of freedom, the quantile is approximately 2.306. This\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>52<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mn>4.612<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">52\\pm4.612<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>47.388<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>56.612<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[47.388;56.612]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns. The interval estimates the mean, not the duration of a future\nindividual operation; the latter can vary much more widely. For very\nsmall, skewed samples or dependent data, the t-method is not\nautomatically reliable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From the model to a test decision<\/h2>\n<p>To ask whether the true mean differs from 50 s, set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>:<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0:\\mu=50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>:<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_1:\\mu\\ne50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe test statistic is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>52<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=(52-50)/2=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder the null hypothesis the two-sided p-value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.347<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(|T_8|\\ge1)\\approx0.347<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt indicates how often, under\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the model, a test statistic at least as extreme would be expected.\nIt is neither the probability that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis true nor a measure of the size of the effect. At a pre-specified 5%\nlevel,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not rejected here. The fact that 50 lies in the corresponding\ninterval reflects the same decision; equality has not thereby been\nestablished. One-sided hypotheses must be justified on substantive\ngrounds before analysis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>An independent normally distributed sample of 16 measurements has\nmean 24 and standard deviation 4. Test\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>:<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>27<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0:\\mu=27<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nusing a two-sided test at 5%.<\/p>\n<p>Solution: Standard error 1,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=-3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n15 degrees of freedom; the critical quantile is approximately 2.131.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>2.131<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|t|&gt;2.131<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso reject under the model assumptions. The interval\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>21.869<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>26.131<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[21.869;26.131]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexcludes 27.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/eda/section3/eda352.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\ne-Handbook: Confidence Limits for the Mean, 1.3.5.2<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openintrostat.github.io/ims-tutorials/04-foundations/\">OpenIntro:\nFoundations of inference (tutorial overview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M6-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M6-L5 – Type I and type II errors, power and effect size<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Bachelor: Statistical inference and experimental design (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L4<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L7<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Define error rates relative to\nhypotheses and derive a sample size from a target effect.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two possible decision errors<\/h2>\n<p>For a valid test, the significance level\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbounds the probability of incorrectly rejecting the null hypothesis. A\ntype II error, with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta(\\theta)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nconsists of failing to reject a false null hypothesis; it depends on the\nparticular parameter value under the alternative. Power is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\beta(\\theta)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA non-significant result from a low-power test provides little evidence\nagainst a practically important alternative.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How power arises<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001721_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Zwei Normaldichten mit Ablehnungsgrenze 103,29 ms. Rechte Fläche unter Nullverteilung ist Alpha, linke unter Alternativverteilung Beta.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Zwei Normaldichten mit Ablehnungsgrenze\n103,29 ms. Rechte Fläche unter Nullverteilung ist Alpha, linke unter\nAlternativverteilung Beta.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Significance level and power.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Consider independent normally distributed measurements with known\nstandard deviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nms. A one-sided z-test of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>:<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0:\\mu\\le100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nagainst\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>:<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_1:\\mu&gt;100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrejects when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>0.95<\/mn><\/msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar X&gt;100+z_{0.95}\\sigma/\\sqrt n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe boundary is 103.290 ms. Under\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>104<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=104<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe standard deviation of the sample mean is still 2 ms. Therefore<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>104<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>103.290<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>104<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.639<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\beta(104)=1-\\Phi\\left(\\frac{103.290-104}{2}\\right)\\approx0.639.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the cumulative distribution function of the standard normal\ndistribution. The graphic represents the two distributions of the means\nand the different error regions. The smallest meaningful effect must be\nspecified on substantive grounds, for example 4 ms here. Its\nstandardised effect size is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=4/10=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA very small but precisely measured effect can be statistically\nsignificant and practically irrelevant.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sample size planning<\/h2>\n<p>For the described z-test with desired power\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=\\mu_0+\\delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nrearranging the normal quantiles gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge\\left(\\frac{(z_{1-\\alpha}+z_{1-\\beta})\\sigma}{\\delta}\\right)^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\npower 0.8 and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe unrounded quantiles (approximately 1.645 and 0.842) give a value of\n38.64, so at least 39 independent measurements are needed. This is a\nmodel-based calculation for a one-sided test with known variance. With\nunknown variance, two groups, clustering or sequential decisions, the\nplanning must be adapted. Dropouts should also be accounted for. For\npractically equivalent outcomes, specifically designed equivalence tests\nwith a pre-defined tolerance may be appropriate; an ordinary\nnon-significant test is insufficient.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>How does the minimum number calculated above change if the intended\ndetectable effect is only 2 ms rather than 4 ms?<\/p>\n<p>Solution: The unrounded requirement quadruples, to approximately\n154.56; rounding up gives 155. Doubling the noise while keeping the\neffect unchanged produces the same factor.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.openintro.org/book/os/\">OpenIntro Statistics:\nChapters 1, 5, 6 and 7 (chapter overview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openintrostat.github.io/ims-tutorials/04-foundations/\">OpenIntro:\nFoundations of inference (tutorial overview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M6-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M6-L6 – Experimental design, randomisation and A/B tests<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Bachelor: Statistical inference and experimental design (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L4<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Specify the randomisation unit,\noutcome measure and analysis plan of an A/B experiment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What should be changed?<\/h2>\n<p>A software team wants to test whether a revised search function more\noften helps users find a desired document. Version A remains unchanged;\nversion B differs only in the intended feature. The target population,\nsuccess criterion (e.g. at least one suitable document within 60 s),\ntime period and smallest relevant effect must be determined in advance.\nOther measures such as crashes or processing time may serve as guardrail\nmetrics. The result of a test applies first to the defined population\nand the observed usage scenario.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Allocation and comparability<\/h2>\n<p>Participants are randomly allocated to the variants in a 1:1 ratio.\nThe randomisation unit here is the person, not the individual request;\nall sessions of one person remain assigned to the same variant. Random\nallocation balances known and unknown influencing factors in\nexpectation, but does not guarantee identical groups. Block\nrandomisation can improve balance, for example by experience level.\nWhere interactions between participants are expected, allocation of\nentire groups with an appropriate analysis should be considered.\nSelection from the target population and assignment to variants are two\ndistinct mechanisms (<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L1<\/a>).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Synthetic A/B test<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001501_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Zufallszuteilung von 800 Personen auf zwei Gruppen. A: 160 von 400 erfolgreich; B: 188 von 400; Differenz 7 Prozentpunkte.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Zufallszuteilung von 800 Personen auf\nzwei Gruppen. A: 160 von 400 erfolgreich; B: 188 von 400; Differenz 7\nProzentpunkte.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Randomised A/B test.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Four hundred people each use A or B. In A, 160 succeed; in B, 188.\nThe estimated rates are 40% and 47%; the absolute difference is 7\npercentage points and the relative increase is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.07<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.40<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>17.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.07/0.40=17.5\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor independent binary outcomes, the standard error of the difference\ncan be approximated by<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat{SE}=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\hat p_A(1-\\hat p_A)}{n_A}+\\frac{\\hat p_B(1-\\hat p_B)}{n_B}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here it is approximately 0.03497, giving a Wald interval for\nindependent groups of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.07<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mn>1.96<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.03497<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.07\\pm1.96\\cdot0.03497<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor about 0.15 to 13.85 percentage points. The graphic connects\nallocation, counts and the difference. The interval is unsuitable for\nsmall samples or extreme rates; more robust interval procedures may then\nbe considered. Unobserved outcomes must be reported separately; they are\nnot automatically unsuccessful requests.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Specify the plan before starting<\/h2>\n<p>Planning includes sample size, duration, exclusions, primary metric,\nanalysis method and handling of missing data. Arbitrarily stopping after\nthe first significant result invalidates the usual guarantees of a\nfixed-sample test. Pre-defined sequential methods account for repeated\nanalyses. An intention-to-treat analysis compares groups according to\ntheir original assignment in principle; the handling of missing outcomes\nmust still be planned. For practical implementation, the estimated\nbenefit should also be weighed against operating costs, risks and\nuncertainty.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>For a new reading mode, family members may share devices. Why might\nrandom assignment of individual sessions be problematic?<\/p>\n<p>Solution: The same person may experience several variants; learning\neffects and shared devices make outcomes dependent. Stable assignment by\nperson or, where appropriate, assignment by household with suitable\nanalysis avoids some of these problems.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.openintro.org/book/os/\">OpenIntro Statistics:\nChapters 1, 5, 6 and 7 (chapter overview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openintrostat.github.io/ims-tutorials/04-foundations/\">OpenIntro:\nFoundations of inference (tutorial overview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M6-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M6-L7 – Multiple testing and false discoveries<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Bachelor: Statistical inference and experimental design (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L6<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L4<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish the family-wise\nerror rate from the false discovery rate and apply corrections.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When many questions are tested at once<\/h2>\n<p>A sensor database is searched for 20 possible associations, although\nall null hypotheses are true. If the tests are independent and each\nrejects with probability 0.05, the probability of at least one false\nrejection is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>0.95<\/mn><mn>20<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.642<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-0.95^{20}\\approx0.642<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe expected number of false rejections is 20 times 0.05, or 1. These\nare different quantities. Tests across different time windows or\nrepeatedly trying different analyses can also constitute a family of\ntests.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Protecting the entire family<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the number of false rejections and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe number of all rejections. The family-wise error rate (FWER) is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(V\\ge1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Bonferroni rule tests each of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhypotheses at level\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha/m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe union bound gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(V\\ge1)\\le m(\\alpha/m)=\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif the individual tests provide valid p-values; independence is not\nrequired. With five tests and a level of 0.05, the threshold is 0.01.\nHolm’s method compares p-values sorted in ascending order successively\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha/(m-i+1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand stops at the first exceedance; it rejects at least as many\nhypotheses as Bonferroni with the same FWER guarantee.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Benjamini–Hochberg: a proportion rather than an event<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001643_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Fünf sortierte p-Werte und zwei Korrekturgrenzen. Benjamini-Hochberg verwirft Ränge 1 und 2, Bonferroni nur Rang 1.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Fünf sortierte p-Werte und zwei\nKorrekturgrenzen. Benjamini-Hochberg verwirft Ränge 1 und 2, Bonferroni\nnur Rang 1.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Thresholds for five simultaneous tests.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The false discovery rate is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[V/\\max(R,1)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the Benjamini–Hochberg procedure (BH), sort the p-values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>⋯<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{(1)}\\le\\cdots\\le p_{(m)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand find the largest\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor which\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{(k)}\\le kq/m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nReject all hypotheses with ranks up to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nif no such rank exists, reject none. FDR control holds under\nindependence or certain positive dependence structures with valid\np-values. Under general dependence, a more conservative adjustment such\nas Benjamini–Yekutieli may be used.<\/p>\n<p>The five p-values 0.004, 0.018, 0.033, 0.041 and 0.20 give thresholds\n0.01, 0.02, 0.03, 0.04 and 0.05 at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe last qualifying rank is 2, so the first two hypotheses are rejected.\nBonferroni rejects only the first. The graphic shows the sorted values\nand both threshold lines. An FDR of 5% does not mean that exactly 5% of\nthe selected results in any single list are false.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Three p-values are 0.02, 0.025 and 0.90. Which hypotheses does BH\nreject at 5%?<\/p>\n<p>Solution: The thresholds are approximately 0.0167, 0.0333 and 0.05.\nRank 2 meets the condition, so ranks 1 and 2 are rejected even though\nrank 1 exceeds its own threshold. The rule is a step-up procedure and\nmust not stop at the first exceedance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://search.r-project.org/R/refmans/stats/help/p.adjust.html\">R\nstats: Adjust P-values for Multiple Comparisons<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.stats.false_discovery_control.html\">SciPy:\nfalse_discovery_control (reference)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M6-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M6-L8 – Bootstrap and permutation tests<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Bachelor: Statistical inference and experimental design (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L8<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L2<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish resampling for\nuncertainty from resampling for a null distribution and apply both\nreproducibly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Samples from the sample<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose four independently sampled processing times are 2, 4, 6 and 8\ns. In the non-parametric bootstrap, four values are repeatedly drawn\nwith replacement from these four observations. In one repetition, some\nvalues may occur several times and others may be absent, for example 2,\n2, 6, 8 with mean 4.5. The original sample is used as an empirical\nmodel. The spread of the repeatedly calculated statistic approximates\nits standard error; quantiles can form a bootstrap percentile interval.\nThis simple interval can be unreliable for small samples or strongly\nbiased or non-regular statistics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Permuting under a null hypothesis<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001663_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Häufigkeit der Differenzen über 20 Gruppenzuteilungen; nur die beiden Randfälle minus und plus 6 Sekunden sind mindestens so extrem wie beobachtet.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Häufigkeit der Differenzen über 20\nGruppenzuteilungen; nur die beiden Randfälle minus und plus 6 Sekunden\nsind mindestens so extrem wie beobachtet.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Exact permutation distribution.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Two randomly assigned groups yield A=(2,4,6), B=(8,10,12) s. The\ndifference B minus A is 6 s. Under the sharp null hypothesis that no\nunit is affected by assignment, and with completely random assignment of\nthree units to each group, all\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mn>6<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\binom63=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngroup assignments are equally probable. Exactly two give an absolute\ndifference in means of at least 6 s. The exact two-sided p-value is\ntherefore 2/20=0.10. In a general permutation test, the data must be\nexchangeable under the null hypothesis with respect to the permitted\npermutations. An arbitrary test of equal means under unequal\ndistributions does not automatically meet this condition. The graphic\nshows the frequencies of the 20 differences; the two boundary cases\nbelong to the extreme set.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Computational experiment with Python<\/h2>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> itertools <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> combinations<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rng <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"dv\">42<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>boot <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> rng.choice(x, size<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">10000<\/span>, <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(x)), replace<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>).mean(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(boot.std(ddof<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>), <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>diffs <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> []<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> idx <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> combinations(<span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(y)), <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    mask <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.zeros(<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(y), dtype<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">bool<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    mask[<span class=\"bu\">list<\/span>(idx)] <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"va\">True<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    diffs.append(y[<span class=\"op\">~<\/span>mask].mean() <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> y[mask].mean())<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(np.mean(np.<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(diffs) <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span> <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-12<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The theoretical bootstrap standard deviation of the mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.118<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{5/4}\\approx1.118<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns; the simulation should be close to it. The second output value is 0.1.\nWhen permutations are simulated randomly rather than enumerated\nexhaustively, the Monte Carlo p-value can be calculated using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(b+1)/(B+1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsimulated statistics are at least as extreme as the observed one. The\nadded ones prevent an apparently exact zero in a finite simulation and\naccount for the observed case.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limitations, exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>For time series or multiple measurements per device, independent\ndraws of individual observations are usually an incorrect resampling\nmodel. Depending on the structure, block or cluster bootstrap and\nrestricted permutations may be appropriate. In a paired study, A and B\nare measured for each device. How should the paired structure be taken\ninto account?<\/p>\n<p>Solution: In the bootstrap, draw complete device pairs together; in a\nsuitable paired randomisation test, swap the treatment labels within a\npair in accordance with the actual assignment design. Unrestricted\nmixing of all individual measurements destroys the dependencies within\npairs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openintrostat.github.io/ims-tutorials/04-foundations/\">OpenIntro:\nFoundations of inference (tutorial overview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.openintro.org/book/os/\">OpenIntro Statistics:\nChapters 1, 5, 6 and 7 (chapter overview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
